{"name":"Afternoon Tea","hex":"#594e40","fetched":"2026-10-20","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"afternoon tea","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A formal afternoon meal comprising light snacks, accompanied by tea.","A small meal or snack eaten between lunch and dinner (supper); a period of time set aside for this purpose, taken as a break from work or from a conference."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/afternoon_tea"}],"entries":[{"title":"Afternoon Tea","blocks":[{"text":"Afternoon Tea is a 1910 oil painting by American artist Richard E. Miller, located in the Indianapolis Museum of Art. Like many of Miller's paintings, it depicts women in a sunny scene, filled with flowers, depicted in his Impressionist style with a distinct flavor of Japonism."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Afternoon_Tea_by_Richard_Emil_Miller.jpg/330px-Afternoon_Tea_by_Richard_Emil_Miller.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afternoon_Tea","wikidata":"Q16960444"},{"title":"Afternoon Tea (Bracquemond)","blocks":[{"text":"Afternoon Tea is an 1880 oil-on-canvas painting by French artist Marie Bracquemond. It is a portrait of the artist's half-sister Louise Quivoron, who often served as a model for her paintings, reading in a garden at Bracquemond's home in the Parisian suburb of Sèvres. The work was shown during an exhibition in 1919 and purchased by the French government from Bracquemond's son Pierre. It is now in the collection of the Petit Palais, Musée des Beaux-Arts de la Ville de Paris. It is one of her few works held in a public collection. The theme of the work reflects the intellectual activities of the lives of women, and can be compared to related works such as Mary Cassatt’s The Reader (1877) and Harriet Backer’s Evening (1890)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Braquemond_Afternoon_Tea.jpg/330px-Braquemond_Afternoon_Tea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afternoon_Tea_(Bracquemond)","wikidata":"Q15877748"},{"title":"Tea (meal)","blocks":[{"text":"Tea is an umbrella term for several different meals consisting of food sometimes accompanied by tea to drink. The English writer Isabella Beeton, whose books on home economics were widely read in the 19th century, describes meals of various kinds and provides menus for the \"old-fashioned tea\", the \"at-home tea\", the \"family tea\", and the \"high tea\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Afternoon_meal_in_Tiffin.jpg/330px-Afternoon_meal_in_Tiffin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tea_(meal)","wikidata":"Q245009"},{"title":"Afternoon","blocks":[{"text":"Afternoon is the period from noon to sunset or evening. It is the time when the sun is descending from its peak in the sky to somewhat before its terminus at the horizon in the west. In human life, it occupies roughly the latter half of the standard work and school day. In literal terms, it refers to a time specifically after noon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Wien_T%C3%BCrkenschanzpark_%282376611600%29.jpg/330px-Wien_T%C3%BCrkenschanzpark_%282376611600%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afternoon","wikidata":"Q283102"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Afternoon_Tea_by_Richard_Emil_Miller.jpg/500px-Afternoon_Tea_by_Richard_Emil_Miller.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Afternoon_Tea_by_Richard_Emil_Miller.jpg/500px-Afternoon_Tea_by_Richard_Emil_Miller.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"